  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 205 AM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nThursday through Friday night, the upper ridge over the Central\nCONUS is forecast to slowly shift eastward and flatten out due \nto shortwave energy passing through the flow to our north. This \nwill result in high pressure moving across the area at the surface \nand aloft through the end of the week, leading to quiet and seasonal \nweather. Temperatures will remain in the 80s, with lower humidity \nand mostly clear skies. Winds will also remain light and variable \nthrough this timeframe, bringing quite the pleasant end to the work \nweek.\n\nSaturday and beyond, the upper ridge finally gets pushed east \nof the area. With high pressure to the east, the flow will turn\nto the south and draw warm air and moisture northward into the\narea into early next week. Upper level flow will generally be \nzonal to southwesterly, which will put us near the main storm \ntrack. Thus, if this pattern holds true, it looks like we are \nheading into a potentially active stretch of weather late this \nweekend and into next week. Global models are in general \nagreement on chances for precipitation, with some differences in\nthe timing and track of these individual shortwaves. Thus, some\nthings will need to get ironed out as we get closer. Although, \nNBM does introduce daily PoPs (20-40%) Sunday into the middle of\nnext week. It is too soon to determine any severe threat with \nthese shortwaves.",
